// D0160 — PROPOSED. Browser-level console testing: the human asked whether Playwright would have caught
// issue141, and it would. This records the ladder, recommends a rung, and leaves the toolchain call to them
// because it cuts against the buildless/offline ethos D0118 and D0048 both rest on.
// #ProspectiveChange (D0070): proposes a new verification lane; changes no schema and no process definition.
package Decision0160 {
    private import EngineElement::*;
    private import EngineWork::*;
    private import EngineVerification::*;
    private import EngineRelationships::*;

    #ProspectiveChange part d0160 : Decision {
        :>> id = "7c41e9b5-2d38-4a06-b19f-58e0a3c7f412";
        :>> title = "D0160: how the console gets tested - a text guard now, a DOM or browser lane behind a human call";
        :>> createdAt = "2026-08-17";
        :>> createdBy = "claudeOpus5";
        :>> status = DecisionStatus::proposed;
        :>> context = "I CLAIMED SOMETHING FALSE AND THE HUMAN CAUGHT IT. Closing issue141 I recorded that 'no automated check in this engine can read a browser, so there is no gate that would have caught a DOM index bug and none that would catch the next one'. They asked: playwright wouldnt fix? It would. The claim conflated an ABSENCE (this repository has no browser test, no package.json, no node_modules, and not one test that reads the console asset) with an IMPOSSIBILITY, which is the exact error I corrected three times during the critique backfill - asserting a defect or a gap after looking in one place. MEASURED STATE: the console is 470 lines of JavaScript embedded by include_str, reachable by 12 serve tests none of which touch it, and its two defects this week were both found by the human using it - the pipe that killed the server, and the off-by-four card binding.";
        :>> decision = "PROPOSED, as a ladder with one rung already built. RUNG 1, DONE THIS SPRINT, no toolchain: a Rust test over the include_str-embedded CONSOLE_HTML asserting the console binds card targets by data-* identity and contains no positional `.c` group selection. It catches the anti-pattern that produced issue141 and it is proven in the failing direction. RUNG 2, RECOMMENDED, small toolchain: a DOM-level test (jsdom or linkedom - Node, no browser binary) that loads the asset, stubs fetch, dispatches a click on each card and asserts the destination - this catches WRONG BEHAVIOUR generally, not just one anti-pattern. RUNG 3, the human's suggestion: Playwright, a real browser, which additionally catches rendering, layout and the interactions rungs 1 and 2 cannot see. WHERE IT RUNS IS THE LOAD-BEARING PART: rungs 2 and 3 belong in CI and stay OPT-IN locally, exactly as D0112 demoted the JVM kernel to an optional deep-semantics backstop rather than a local gate. keel init must not require Node, and `keel gate --fast` must not download a browser.";
        :>> rationale = "WHY NOT PLAYWRIGHT ON THE LOCAL GATE, which is the only part I would argue against: this project is deliberately toolchain-free. D0118 chose a buildless SPA with vendored dependencies and no CDN; D0048 requires validation-path tools to be kernel-free where possible, and that same reasoning demoted the JVM to opt-in after issue081 showed an all-or-nothing heavy oracle gets bypassed wholesale. A browser download on the commit path invites the same bypass. WHY RUNG 2 IS THE RECOMMENDATION: it buys the general case (behaviour, not one pattern) for a dependency that is text-only and vendorable, and jsdom can run the exact click path this console uses since the binding is now a delegated listener on a container. WHY RUNG 1 WAS BUILT WITHOUT ASKING: it needs no decision - it is a unit test over an embedded string, it would have caught the actual defect, and declining to build it while proposing a heavier lane would have left the cheap control unbuilt for the duration of a conversation. THE COST OF RUNG 1 IS ITS CEILING and is stated in the test itself: a text assertion catches a named anti-pattern, not a wrong destination that is bound correctly.";
        :>> consequences = "If ACCEPTED at rung 2 or 3: a Node dev-dependency enters the repository, CI gains a job, and the console acquires its first behavioural coverage - which is where both of this week's defects would have been caught. If REJECTED: rung 1 stands as the only console control, the honest reading is that console behaviour is verified by the human using it, and that should be said in the console's own requirements rather than left implicit. EITHER WAY, one correction is already made: the false claim is corrected in issue141 and in sprint 339's review gate, with what I asserted, why it was wrong, and who caught it.";
    }
}
